      annotations: [
        {
          id: 'a7.17.1',
          kind: "reading",
          refs: ['g7.17.3', 'g7.17.7', 'g7.17.8'],
          lemma: undefined,
          title: "The test of truth is the will",
          body: "The authorities want Jesus’ *credentials* — where did an untrained man learn this (v. 15)? He answers with a claim about *how truth is known*, and it is one of the most searching sentences in the Gospel: **“If anyone wants to do God’s will, he will know whether the teaching is from God.”** Truth here is not settled first in the head and then, perhaps, obeyed; it is disclosed *to the willing*. The person who genuinely intends to do God’s will — who comes ready to *obey* whatever they find — will recognise Jesus’ words as God’s; the person who has already decided to keep hold of their own life will always find a reason not to. This is why the same evidence divides a crowd (vv. 12, 43): the sticking point is rarely a missing argument but an unwilling heart. It cuts against the modern instinct that neutral detachment is the surest path to truth about God — Jesus says the surest path is a will bent toward obedience. Doubt, on this reading, is often less an intellectual problem than a moral posture; and honest seeking, the readiness to *do* before you fully *see*, is itself the beginning of sight.",
        },
      ],
