      annotations: [
        {
          id: 'a7.37.1',
          kind: "reading",
          refs: ['g7.37.18', 'g7.38.9', 'g7.38.16'],
          lemma: undefined,
          title: "Rivers of living water",
          body: "This is the climax of the feast, and Jesus times it perfectly. On **the great last day**, just when the priest has poured out the water drawn from Siloam — the whole rite a prayer for rain and a memory of water from the rock in the desert — Jesus **stands and shouts** over the crowd: *if anyone thirsts, come to me and drink.* He is not commenting on the ceremony; he is claiming to *be* what it enacts. Back at the well he offered the Samaritan woman a spring welling up within (4:14); here the promise overflows outward — **“out of his heart will flow rivers of living water,”** not a private trickle but currents that run *through* a person to others. John, unusually, tells us plainly what it means (v. 39): the living water is **the Spirit**, whom believers *would* receive — but *“as yet the Spirit was not given, because Jesus was not yet glorified.”* The gift waits on the cross and the resurrection; the rivers begin to flow only from a Christ lifted up (cf. 19:34, the water from his side; 20:22, “receive the Holy Spirit”). So the invitation stands over every dry and thirsty life — *come and drink* — and the promise is not merely to have your own thirst quenched but to become, by the indwelling Spirit, a channel through which that water reaches a parched world.",
        },
      ],
