      annotations: [
        {
          id: 'a7.42.1',
          kind: "concept",
          refs: ['g7.42.9', 'g7.42.12'],
          lemma: "ἐκ … Δαυεὶδ … ἀπὸ Βηθλεέμ",
          title: "The Bethlehem irony",
          body: "They are exactly right about the prophecy — the Christ comes from **David’s line** and from **Bethlehem** (Mic 5:2; 2 Sam 7) — and exactly wrong about Jesus, whom they write off as a Galilean. The reader knows what they do not: he *was* born in Bethlehem, of David’s house. John doesn’t bother to correct them; he lets the irony hang, trusting his readers to see that the crowd condemns Jesus by a Scripture he in fact fulfils. Their confident knowledge of the Bible becomes the very thing that trips them.",
        },
      ],
